1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,640
In the early 2000s, mobile operators

2
00:00:02,870 --> 00:00:06,540
were selling data at a price
that sounds absurd today.

3
00:00:07,136 --> 00:00:09,716
Not mobile internet. Text messages.

4
00:00:10,560 --> 00:00:14,380
A single text message carried
at most 140 bytes.

5
00:00:15,093 --> 00:00:17,753
At ten cents a message,
one megabyte of texts

6
00:00:17,983 --> 00:00:20,820
would cost you more
than seven hundred dollars.

7
00:00:20,920 --> 00:00:21,840
And people paid it.

8
00:00:22,070 --> 00:00:24,420
Billions of times a day.

9
00:00:25,050 --> 00:00:29,350
So how did a tiny side feature
of the mobile network become a gold mine?

10
00:00:29,610 --> 00:00:33,810
And why was every message
limited to exactly 160 characters?

11
00:00:36,650 --> 00:00:39,690
Let's start with that number,
because it has two parents.

12
00:00:40,415 --> 00:00:43,075
In 1985, a German engineer

13
00:00:43,305 --> 00:00:46,635
called Friedhelm Hillebrand
sat down at a typewriter.

14
00:00:47,317 --> 00:00:51,797
He typed out ordinary sentences
and questions, and counted the characters.

15
00:00:52,661 --> 00:00:54,601
Almost all of them fit into 160.

16
00:00:56,383 --> 00:00:59,383
He checked postcards
and telex messages too.

17
00:01:00,083 --> 00:01:03,403
Most of those were shorter
than 160 as well.

18
00:01:03,969 --> 00:01:05,449
So the first parent is human.

19
00:01:05,679 --> 00:01:11,109
160 characters was enough
to say most things people actually say.

20
00:01:12,326 --> 00:01:14,426
The second parent is the network.

21
00:01:15,302 --> 00:01:17,642
Engineers were designing GSM,

22
00:01:17,722 --> 00:01:21,042
the new European standard
for digital mobile phones.

23
00:01:21,498 --> 00:01:24,018
And a phone doesn't only carry your voice.

24
00:01:24,658 --> 00:01:27,978
It constantly exchanges small messages
with the network.

25
00:01:28,764 --> 00:01:33,144
I'm here. I've moved to another cell.
A call is coming in.

26
00:01:33,705 --> 00:01:35,185
That's called signalling.

27
00:01:35,265 --> 00:01:38,765
And most of the time,
those channels sit almost empty.

28
00:01:39,285 --> 00:01:41,085
So the idea was modest.

29
00:01:41,825 --> 00:01:45,545
What if short text messages
could ride along in that spare capacity?

30
00:01:46,244 --> 00:01:48,364
But a signalling message is small.

31
00:01:49,024 --> 00:01:51,924
The room left for the text was 140 bytes.

32
00:01:52,699 --> 00:01:56,459
140 bytes is 1120 bits.

33
00:01:57,169 --> 00:02:00,049
Simple text doesn't need
eight bits per character.

34
00:02:00,689 --> 00:02:02,449
GSM used seven.

35
00:02:02,669 --> 00:02:07,269
And 1120 divided by seven is exactly 160.

36
00:02:08,803 --> 00:02:10,403
It wasn't a coincidence.

37
00:02:11,123 --> 00:02:15,183
The first design had room
for only about 146 characters.

38
00:02:15,403 --> 00:02:19,483
So the engineers stretched it
until 160 fitted.

39
00:02:21,983 --> 00:02:23,463
There's a catch, though.

40
00:02:23,603 --> 00:02:27,123
Seven bits only give you
a hundred and twenty-eight characters.

41
00:02:27,590 --> 00:02:30,870
That covers English,
and a handful of accented letters.

42
00:02:31,275 --> 00:02:33,402
But not the Czech ř.

43
00:02:33,515 --> 00:02:38,975
Not the Polish ł, the Hungarian ő,
or the Cyrillic alphabet.

44
00:02:39,864 --> 00:02:42,384
Use one of those,
and the phone switches to an encoding

45
00:02:42,614 --> 00:02:44,784
with sixteen bits per character.

46
00:02:45,043 --> 00:02:49,183
And the same 140 bytes
now hold only 70 characters.

47
00:02:49,960 --> 00:02:52,960
One letter could cut your message
by more than half.

48
00:02:53,219 --> 00:02:54,719
Which is why, for years,

49
00:02:54,799 --> 00:02:58,199
millions of people wrote their texts
without accents at all.

50
00:03:00,809 --> 00:03:05,289
The service itself was called
SMS. Short Message Service.

51
00:03:05,748 --> 00:03:09,008
And nobody expected it
to become the way people talk.

52
00:03:09,684 --> 00:03:12,244
It was a small extra
built into the network.

53
00:03:12,644 --> 00:03:16,604
Something like a pager:
a short note for someone on the move.

54
00:03:17,691 --> 00:03:20,351
On the 3rd of December 1992,

55
00:03:20,451 --> 00:03:23,591
the first text message was sent
across a British network.

56
00:03:24,358 --> 00:03:26,078
It said: "Merry Christmas."

57
00:03:26,886 --> 00:03:29,426
It was typed on a computer, not a phone.

58
00:03:30,118 --> 00:03:32,938
The phone that received it
could display the message.

59
00:03:33,018 --> 00:03:35,798
But it had no way to send one back.

60
00:03:37,958 --> 00:03:41,118
Getting a message to someone
worked like a post office.

61
00:03:41,851 --> 00:03:45,671
Your phone handed the text to a server
in the network, the SMS Centre.

62
00:03:46,639 --> 00:03:50,119
If the recipient's phone was switched off,
the message waited.

63
00:03:50,853 --> 00:03:52,853
Out of coverage? It waited.

64
00:03:53,401 --> 00:03:56,441
And when the phone came back,
the network tried again.

65
00:03:57,038 --> 00:03:59,298
Engineers call it store and forward.

66
00:03:59,978 --> 00:04:02,238
No internet, no account, no app.

67
00:04:02,638 --> 00:04:04,218
Just a mobile signal.

68
00:04:06,528 --> 00:04:10,148
Then phones learned to send texts,
and people discovered something

69
00:04:10,378 --> 00:04:12,828
the designers hadn't really planned for.

70
00:04:13,344 --> 00:04:16,344
A phone call needs
both people at the same time.

71
00:04:16,939 --> 00:04:18,899
A text doesn't need that.

72
00:04:19,573 --> 00:04:20,893
You could send "Where are you?"

73
00:04:21,123 --> 00:04:23,373
and carry on with your day.

74
00:04:24,023 --> 00:04:27,403
The other person could answer
in a minute, or in an hour.

75
00:04:28,002 --> 00:04:30,842
And on a keypad
with three or four letters on each key,

76
00:04:30,922 --> 00:04:32,882
people got astonishingly fast.

77
00:04:33,666 --> 00:04:36,086
The phone stopped being just a telephone.

78
00:04:36,806 --> 00:04:39,126
It became a personal messaging device.

79
00:04:40,572 --> 00:04:42,772
And that's where the gold mine opened.

80
00:04:44,902 --> 00:04:47,802
Think about what a text message
costs the network.

81
00:04:48,603 --> 00:04:50,503
The towers were already there.

82
00:04:50,963 --> 00:04:53,263
The signalling channels
were already there.

83
00:04:53,464 --> 00:04:56,684
And each message was
at most a hundred and forty bytes.

84
00:04:57,243 --> 00:05:01,243
But customers didn't pay for bytes.
They paid per message.

85
00:05:01,570 --> 00:05:04,810
Or for a bundle
of a few hundred messages a month.

86
00:05:05,442 --> 00:05:08,362
Prices varied a lot
from country to country.

87
00:05:09,058 --> 00:05:12,078
In much of Europe,
you paid for every single text.

88
00:05:12,781 --> 00:05:15,701
In the United States,
texting caught on later,

89
00:05:15,941 --> 00:05:18,361
and you often paid to receive a text, too.

90
00:05:18,978 --> 00:05:22,278
But the basic arithmetic
was the same almost everywhere.

91
00:05:23,021 --> 00:05:26,421
A tiny amount of data,
sold as a separate service.

92
00:05:26,749 --> 00:05:29,049
And sold billions of times a day.

93
00:05:29,703 --> 00:05:34,083
For operators, it became one of the most
profitable things they had ever sold.

94
00:05:37,113 --> 00:05:39,033
And then it got a little strange.

95
00:05:39,897 --> 00:05:43,197
Some operators offered free texting
from their own websites.

96
00:05:44,017 --> 00:05:46,417
Type a number, type a message, send.

97
00:05:47,035 --> 00:05:48,455
Programmers noticed.

98
00:05:49,255 --> 00:05:52,795
If a website can send a text,
a program can use that website.

99
00:05:53,640 --> 00:05:56,100
Soon there were apps
that sent texts from your computer,

100
00:05:56,330 --> 00:05:58,940
through the operators' own gateways.

101
00:05:59,645 --> 00:06:04,085
The service people paid for on their
phones was sometimes free one window away.

102
00:06:06,735 --> 00:06:09,815
But 160 characters had another limit.

103
00:06:10,479 --> 00:06:12,539
What if you wanted to send a picture?

104
00:06:13,349 --> 00:06:17,209
Enter MMS. Multimedia Messaging Service.

105
00:06:17,927 --> 00:06:20,947
Pictures, sound,
short videos, longer text.

106
00:06:21,628 --> 00:06:23,128
And here's the clever part.

107
00:06:23,208 --> 00:06:26,108
An MMS didn't travel like a text.

108
00:06:26,867 --> 00:06:29,647
First, your phone received
a small hidden message.

109
00:06:30,427 --> 00:06:33,267
Something like:
there's a picture waiting for you.

110
00:06:33,392 --> 00:06:36,172
Then the phone went online
and downloaded it.

111
00:06:36,452 --> 00:06:39,292
Which meant MMS needed mobile data.

112
00:06:40,025 --> 00:06:42,665
It needed the right settings
from your operator.

113
00:06:43,149 --> 00:06:47,429
And it had size limits that squeezed
photos into a blurry little rectangle.

114
00:06:48,131 --> 00:06:49,891
It was often expensive.

115
00:06:50,431 --> 00:06:52,631
And sometimes, it simply didn't arrive.

116
00:06:53,434 --> 00:06:56,234
MMS was the awkward teenager
between the text message

117
00:06:56,464 --> 00:06:58,874
and everything that came next.

118
00:06:59,325 --> 00:07:02,205
And what came next
was the internet on your phone.

119
00:07:03,455 --> 00:07:06,295
Once phones had a reliable
data connection,

120
00:07:06,475 --> 00:07:10,155
a message didn't need the operator's
messaging service at all.

121
00:07:10,677 --> 00:07:12,877
It could travel as ordinary data.

122
00:07:13,738 --> 00:07:16,818
Apps like WhatsApp
and Facebook Messenger sent texts,

123
00:07:16,898 --> 00:07:20,418
pictures, voice messages
and videos the same way.

124
00:07:20,895 --> 00:07:23,295
The economics of messaging flipped.

125
00:07:23,678 --> 00:07:25,998
Instead of one message, one charge,

126
00:07:26,261 --> 00:07:30,921
thousands of messages became
a rounding error inside your data plan.

127
00:07:31,541 --> 00:07:33,541
The gold mine didn't run dry.

128
00:07:33,861 --> 00:07:35,601
It was simply bypassed.

129
00:07:38,091 --> 00:07:40,231
But SMS didn't disappear.

130
00:07:40,667 --> 00:07:44,627
Because it still has one trick
that most internet messengers don't.

131
00:07:45,093 --> 00:07:46,233
Mobile signal: yes.

132
00:07:46,463 --> 00:07:48,013
Wi-Fi: no.

133
00:07:48,113 --> 00:07:49,733
Mobile data: no.

134
00:07:50,355 --> 00:07:52,735
Even then, SMS still works.

135
00:07:53,416 --> 00:07:56,816
That's why it carries the things
that can't wait for a good connection.

136
00:07:57,672 --> 00:08:01,192
Login codes. Bank alerts.
Delivery updates.

137
00:08:01,793 --> 00:08:03,613
And the occasional "Call me."

138
00:08:05,763 --> 00:08:09,883
The mobile industry did try
to give the old system a modern successor.

139
00:08:10,659 --> 00:08:12,379
It's called RCS.

140
00:08:12,819 --> 00:08:14,739
Rich Communication Services.

141
00:08:15,621 --> 00:08:21,721
Typing indicators. Read receipts.
Group chats. Proper photos. Reactions.

142
00:08:22,655 --> 00:08:26,835
Basically, everything people had been
getting from messaging apps for years.

143
00:08:27,508 --> 00:08:30,228
But RCS needs an internet connection.

144
00:08:30,553 --> 00:08:33,773
So when the data is gone,
the most modern system steps aside,

145
00:08:34,003 --> 00:08:37,903
and the phone can fall back
to the oldest one.

146
00:08:39,603 --> 00:08:43,163
A message designed in the 1980s,
squeezed into the gaps

147
00:08:43,393 --> 00:08:47,463
of a signalling channel,
limited to 160 characters.

148
00:08:48,275 --> 00:08:49,635
It became a gold mine.

149
00:08:50,761 --> 00:08:52,841
It was bypassed by the internet.

150
00:08:53,415 --> 00:08:57,015
And it's still the thing your phone
reaches for when nothing else works.

151
00:09:00,225 --> 00:09:02,685
RCS has one more twist, though.

152
00:09:03,308 --> 00:09:07,128
For years, the best-known phone
in the world simply didn't speak it.

153
00:09:07,449 --> 00:09:09,669
But that's a story for another video.

154
00:09:10,961 --> 00:09:12,781
So here's a question for you.

155
00:09:13,501 --> 00:09:17,421
Was the 160-character limit
a handicap texting survived,

156
00:09:17,501 --> 00:09:19,521
or part of the reason it caught on?

157
00:09:19,861 --> 00:09:21,491
Let me know in the comments.
